[Music]  in 1987 President Ronald Reagan famously  demanded Gorbachev the head of the  Soviet Union to tear down that wall his  speech laid the foundation for this  moment just two years later thousands of  East Germans canned outside the German  Embassy in Hungary demanding access  across the border and three fundamental  things freedom safety and reunification  now I wasn't born yet because of that  moment when I grew up in Berlin there  were no more walls  I never even questioned the right to  safety and freedom and so this happened  four years ago thousands of people were  stranded at the Hungarian border yet  again but this time it wasn't German  citizens who pleaded for the world to  pay attention  it was Syrian refugees this fact a  movement across my country images like  this of people welcoming refugees into  Germany went around the world I was so  proud so I decided to leave my corporate  job in the UK and moved back to Berlin  to support my government in absorbing  the 1 million refugees that ended up  arriving that moment changed my life  since then I've met some incredible  refugees and worked with some of the  most dedicated people I know who try to  support them their strengths and  resilience has become my source of  inspiration and Drive but at the same  time the public conversation has changed  the outpouring of support has turned  into protest and fear instead  tearing down walls we are building them  this scares me I don't fear that we have  lost control of our borders I fear that  we have lost control of our fundamental  values what has happened let's start  with the basics there's 68 million  refugees in the world today these are  people who have been forced to flee from  their homes because of conflict and  violence take Syria a country that has  become synonymous with the world's  refugee crisis the Syrian war started  nine years ago with the Arab Spring  after 13 your boy was tortured and  murdered for writing a pro Arab Spring  graffiti people went out on the streets  to protest peacefully but President  Bashar al-assad  responded with gunfire it's backed one  of the deadliest civil wars in our  modern times leaving 500,000 people  Deb's and 12 million forcefully  displaced that is two-thirds of the  entire country these are people who  stood up to demand freedom safety and  democracy they fight for the values we  preach and we ignore them and so they  literally showed up at our borders at  the height of the crisis sometimes  thousands of refugees came into just  Berlin in a given week on one of my  first days working for the government  there I went to visit an old  decommission airport at the heart of the  city the local state office had resorted  to seizing whatever they could find as  shelters because they simply wasn't any  space then included school gyms and the  old airports for you but what I found  was this if thousands bunk beds  and into what looked like an industrial  hall 2,000 people lived here people from  different cultures different families  from all over the world the echo made  the noise level almost unbearable there  was no peace and quiet no privacy some  of the people who came looking for  freedom and safety had to stay here for  almost two years they were safe  yes but free not really I remember a  social worker who came and pleaded with  us to help a refugee who was severely  disabled and suffered from PTSD placing  him here was cruel not just for him but  really for everyone  imagine trying to sleep whilst he was in  during his night terrors he needed  individual care but we simply weren't  able to provide it if there's one thing  I learned that day it is that no one  chooses this life you placed in it if  you're out of options and there's  something else I learned if you're  facing the humanitarian crisis it forces  you to make trade-offs Hugh gets  prioritized for individual care and who  doesn't he gets to stay and who doesn't  all of these decisions have a tremendous  human cost and not just to the refugees  but really to all of us accommodating  refugees requires a sacrifice and some  of it might be small like getting used  to sharing your public spaces with  people who look really different to you  or giving up your school gym for two  years and some is big like having  government resources diverted away from  your local projects it really is no  wonder that the cycle of his  three always follows the same pattern  when it comes to refugee movements in  1938 67 percent of Americans were  opposed to granting refuge to Jewish  people fleeing Nazi persecution my own  grandma came to Germany as a refugee  she was dremen but she grew up in the  Sudetenland a small strip between  Czechoslovakia and Germany after World  War two all the Germans living there had  a day to pack their bags and leave even  though she shares a nationality and a  language with the people in her new  hometown she was not greeted with  kindness  my point is this is a vicious cycle of  history every generation perpetuates and  this crisis really is no different if we  judge previous generations on whether or  not they took a stance will future  generations not do the same to us I  worry because I know that this sacrifice  is painful what's working in government  I organized a town-hall meeting in one  of the most liberal district of Berlin  where you can find welcomed refugee  posters on every street corner we had to  ask the residents to give up a tiny  piece of their large neighborhood park  for a new refugee shelter turns out the  people were furious I know that we can  take everyone in but that is not what we  are debating right now instead we're  turning a blind eye again we're coming  up with all the creative arguments for  why we don't have the resources to help  why are we not best place to help why in  fact isolation is the best answer  we are back at the vicious cycle of  history I know that we can break it but  for that I'm asking you to make a  sacrifice to stand up for the values I  know we all share and that might not be  today I'm asking you to remember this  talk when the time comes for you to make  a choice do I truly open my arms even if  it costs me or do I look away and that  cost might be small like speaking up  publicly even if it's risky or giving up  its hiney space in your neighborhood  Park or it might be big like going out  of your way to create opportunities for  Refugees in your future companies every  decision every action counts previous  generations might have not gotten it all  right with our countries share history  in literally tearing down walls together  I don't want to be the generation that  builds them back up thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

